course: dictionary
lesson: 11413
title: Things that build up
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Accumulation is a noun. It means the slow collecting or building up of something over time. It can also mean the pile or amount that has built up. Look for clues like «slow», «over time» or «step by step» to help you.
explain: «Snow accumulation on the roof made the shed sag under its weight.» The word tells you the snow piled up little by little until there was a lot of it. You can picture the roof getting heavier and heavier.
explain: «Her savings grew through a steady accumulation of coins each week.» Here accumulation is about money. Each week she added more coins, and they built up into a larger amount.
explain: A good check is to swap in the words «build-up» or «pile». If «a slow build-up of dust» still makes sense, you have found the right meaning.
fact: Accumulation means the slow collecting or building up of something over time.
fact: An accumulation can also be a pile or amount that has built up.
fact: Accumulation is a noun, so you can write «an accumulation» or «accumulations».
fact: In science, you might study the accumulation of snow, rubbish, sediment or dust.
fact: In maths, an accumulation can mean a total that grows step by step.
exercise: «The accumulation of rubbish in the river harmed the fish.» What does accumulation mean here? | A sudden clean-up of the river | Rain falling on the water | *A slow building up of rubbish over time | A fish that swims very fast
exercise: «Her savings grew through a slow accumulation of coins each week.» Which phrase is closest in meaning to accumulation? | A sudden loss of money | *A slow build-up | A bright light | An empty box
exercise: In a maths problem, the total grows a little more each day. Which word best describes this growing total? | A shadow | A sudden drop | *An accumulation | A colour
test: «Snow accumulation on the path made it hard to walk.» What does accumulation mean? | Ice skating | *A building up of snow over time | Snow that melts away quickly | A snowman
test: Which word could replace accumulation in «a slow accumulation of dust»? | Removal | Disappearance | *Build-up | Cleaning
exam: Which sentence uses accumulation correctly? | *A slow build-up of dust on the shelf over many months | A bright red kite flying high | A quick sneeze on the bus | A loud bang from the door
